From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] crypto: rockchip: rk2 - Add RSA on the public key accelerator

The crypto v2 engine carries a public key accelerator (PKA): up to 32
numbers of up to 4096 bits in its 4 KiB SRAM, and opcodes for shifts,
division and modular exponentiation. Rockchip's kernel offers raw RSA on
it; offer the same as an akcipher.

The sequence is Rockchip's: the length and register tables for the
modulus's size, the Montgomery constant NP = 2^(bits(N) + 132) / N
computed by the PKA itself, then MOD_EXP, the result read back once the
PKA is done, longer for a private exponent. Every wait is bounded, the
SRAM's hand-over between the CPU and the PKA included, so a stuck PKA
returns an error instead of spinning. The keys and payloads checked are
generic RSA's: 512 to 4096-bit moduli, an input above 1 and below N - 1,
the output written right-aligned into the destination.

The PKA exponentiates with the private exponent, without CRT: on an
RK3528 it signs 9 times a second at 2048 bits, where the CPU's CRT signs
faster, so it ranks under generic RSA, there for a caller that names it.

Signed-off-by: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
